<h3>What to expect</h3>
<p>This is a <strong>self-paced online course</strong> — there are no scheduled meeting times or fixed deadlines. You move through the material at your own speed. But that flexibility comes with real responsibility. Plan on several hours of reading, writing, and engagement per week — roughly the same commitment as any three-credit-hour college course.</p>

<p>The course is organized into units, and you should complete them in order. Within each unit, the expected flow is: read the overview, work through the lesson pages, complete the assigned reading, then engage with the discussion prompt and take the quiz.</p>

<h4>A few habits that will serve you well</h4>
<div>— Set a regular schedule for yourself — even 30 minutes a day keeps you moving forward</div>
<div>— Read the assigned texts more than once; philosophical writing almost always rewards a second pass</div>
<div>— Take notes as you read and flag moments where an argument seems to break down — those are worth thinking about further</div>
<div>— Give yourself time; don't rush through the material just to finish — the point is to actually engage with the ideas</div>
<div>— Try explaining what you've read to someone not taking the course — if you can't, you probably need another pass</div>
<div>— Contact your instructor by email if you have questions or run into problems</div>

<h4>Technical skills you'll need</h4>
<p>Before you begin, make sure you're comfortable doing the following:</p>
<div>— Navigating a website using a browser (you're already doing this!)</div>
<div>— Downloading, opening, and saving files</div>
<div>— Creating and saving a document in a word processor (Microsoft Word, Google Docs, etc.)</div>
<div>— Using a search engine for basic research</div>

