<h3>What academic honesty means in this course</h3>
<p>Academic dishonesty is, broadly speaking, a form of misrepresentation. When you submit work that isn't genuinely yours, you misrepresent your own understanding — which defeats the purpose of being here. The goal of this course is for you to actually develop the ability to think philosophically. That can't happen if someone else (or something else) does the thinking for you.</p>

<h4>Forms of academic dishonesty</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Cheating</strong> includes copying answers from another student's exam, helping others with exam questions, or using an AI chatbot to write significant portions of an assignment without attribution.</li>
<li><strong>Fabrication</strong> includes making up sources or creating fake data or citations.</li>
<li><strong>Plagiarism</strong> includes submitting someone else's work as your own, copying text from internet sources without proper citation, or submitting AI-generated text as your original work.</li>
</ul>

<h4>On the use of AI tools</h4>
<p>AI writing tools (such as ChatGPT or Claude) may not be used to write or substantially draft your assignments for this course. Submitting AI-generated text as your own work constitutes academic dishonesty.</p>

<p>You <em>may</em> use AI tools to help generate initial ideas, to critique a draft argument you have already written, or to rephrase an awkward sentence — not to produce the argument itself. If you're unsure whether a specific use is acceptable, ask before submitting, not after.</p>

<h4>Consequences</h4>
<p>A first violation results in a warning and a zero on the assignment. A repeat violation results in a failing grade for the course. If you are uncertain about what is and isn't permitted, please ask — that is always the right move.</p>

